?**  \n✅ Yes. The yellow crown-shaped props are visible throughout the concert footage, including during active stage performances (e.g., frames 10–14, 16–20, 21–30). They are held up by fans while performers are on stage, often in sync with the music or lighting.\n\n---\n\n**3) Do crowd members have props during stage events?**  \n✅ Yes. The props are consistently held by audience members during live stage events. For example, in frames 15, 16, 18, 19, and 30, the yellow crowns are raised in unison with the crowd’s energy, indicating they are official or fan-made concert props used to participate in the event.\n\n---\n\n**4) Are people holding items while watching performances?**  \n✅ Yes. In nearly every audience shot (e.g., 1, 3, 4, 5, 7, 15, 24, 25, 26, 28, 30), individuals are seen holding either phones or the yellow crown props while watching the performers. Some are recording, others are waving the props.\n\n---\n\n**5) What handheld items are visible?**  \n✅ The most prominent handheld items are:\n- **Yellow crown-shaped light sticks** (illuminated, plastic, with a handle)\n- **Smartphones** (used for recording or taking photos)\n- **Occasionally, other light sticks or signs**, but the yellow crown is dominant and repeated across frames.\n\n---\n\n**6) Specifically identify the color and shape of objects held.**  \n✅ The props are **bright yellow**, shaped like **crowns** with **three to five pointed tips**, and often have small spherical “jewels” or bulbs on the tips. They are mounted on a handle or base, and many are illuminated from within. This is clearly visible in frames such as:\n- Frame 3: Multiple yellow crowns held aloft.\n- Frame 5: Clear view of the crown shape and yellow color.\n- Frame 19: Crown prop in foreground, performer on stage.\n- Frame 25: Close-up of hand holding yellow crown light stick.\n- Frame 26: Two yellow crowns side-by-side, clearly showing shape and color.\n- Frame 30: Multiple yellow crowns in the crowd, matching the stage lighting.\n\nNo glow sticks (long, tubular, typically unshaped) are visible — the dominant prop is unmistakably the **yellow crown**.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\n✅ The evidence across all 30 frames confirms that **audience members are holding yellow crown-shaped props during the concert**. The props are visible, consistent, and contextually appropriate for a K-pop concert (where such light sticks are common fan merchandise). There is no visual evidence contradicting this.\n\n**Answer ‘1’ (Yellow crown props) is correct.**'}, {'batch_start': 60, 'batch_end': 72, 'image_paths': ['/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2zkJFv-ro4A/frames/frame_1121.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2zkJFv-ro4A/frames/frame_1244.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2zkJFv-ro4A/frames/frame_1245.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2zkJFv-ro4A/frames/frame_1246.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2zkJFv-ro4A/frames/frame_1247.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2zkJFv-ro4A/frames/frame_1248.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2zkJFv-ro4A/frames/frame_1249.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2zkJFv-ro4A/frames/frame_1250.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2zkJFv-ro4A/frames/frame_1251.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2zkJFv-ro4A/frames/frame_1252.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2zkJFv-ro4A/frames/frame_1253.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2zkJFv-ro4A/frames/frame_1254.jpg'], 'response': 'Based on a detailed analysis of the 12 provided concert audience frames, the answer **‘1’ (Yellow crown props)** is **partially correct but misleading without context**. Here’s the breakdown:\n\n---\n\n### 1) Are audience members holding objects?\n✅ **Yes.** In multiple frames (especially 4, 5, 6, 7, 8), audience members are visibly holding up objects.\n\n---\n\n### 2) Are these objects visible during concert performances?\n✅ **Yes.** The objects are clearly visible in frames showing the crowd during active performances (e.g., frames 4–8).\n\n---\n\n### 3) Do crowd members have props during stage events?\n✅ **Yes.** The crowd is actively engaged, waving props during the concert, which is typical of K-pop or large-scale pop concerts.\n\n---\n\n### 4) Are people holding items while watching performances?\n✅ **Yes.** In frames 4–8, many audience members are raising their hands, holding up items toward the stage.\n\n---\n\n### 5) What handheld items are visible?\n✅ **Primarily yellow, crown-shaped light-up props.** In frames 4, 5, 6, 7, and 8, numerous audience members are holding yellow objects shaped like crowns or stylized “V” or “W” shapes — consistent with K-pop fan light sticks or official merchandise. These are **not** generic glow sticks; they have a distinct crown silhouette.\n\n---\n\n### 6) Specifically identify the color and shape of objects held.\n✅ **Color:** Bright yellow/gold.\n✅ **Shape:** Crown-like, with pointed tips or arches — resembling a stylized royal crown or a “V” with extended points. Seen clearly in:\n- Frame 4: Multiple yellow crown-shaped props in the foreground.\n- Frame 5: Crowds holding up yellow objects with crown contours.\n- Frame 6: More yellow props, some blurred but shape discernible.\n- Frame 7: Blurry but yellow crown shapes are visible in motion.\n- Frame 8: A clear yellow crown prop in the foreground, held up near the stage.\n\n---\n\n### Comparison with Other Options (e.g., Glow Sticks)\n❌ **Not standard glow sticks.** The objects are not cylindrical or tubular like typical glow sticks. They are flat, shaped, and illuminated — characteristic of **official K-pop light sticks** (e.g., BTS’s “ARMY Bomb,” EXO’s “EXO-L Lightstick,” etc.), which often feature crown motifs.\n\n---\n\n### Temporal Sequence & Context\n- The props appear consistently from **frame 4 through frame 8**, indicating sustained use during the performance.\n- The stage lighting (yellow/blue spotlights) enhances visibility of the yellow props,